// A shard of the work folder (awg-sync/work-shards.json): the work folder's
// exclusions, without its top-level bar. Written by awg-tools/work-shards.mjs
// and the hub's work-shards-hub.py from projects.stignore - edit that file.

// Boundary of the work folder (awg-projects, C:\AWG).
//
// GOAL: sync the source. Not the third-party packages, not the model weights,
// not the git database.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE TRICK.
//
// Syncthing takes the FIRST matching rule and stops looking. If an include
// such as "!/Github" sits at the top, it expands to "!/github/**" -- "all of
// it, unconditionally" -- and every exclusion further down is never reached.
// That is exactly how 1,002,137 node_modules files landed on the hub on
// 2026-08-05.
//
// So: ALL exclusions first, then the includes, then the bar. Whoever edits
// this file has to keep that order.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHY THIS FOLDER DOES NOT LIVE IN ONEDRIVE
// OneDrive keeps files as placeholders: visible, but empty on disk. Only when
// something reads a file does OneDrive fetch it. Syncthing reads every file
// to check it -- so OneDrive fetches every file. On 2026-08-06 that filled the
// disk, OneDrive freed space, Syncthing asked again, endlessly. The two must
// never share a folder.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* EXCLUDED: .env files, keys, credentials.
// Until 2026-09-02 a "secrets" section kept them off the sync, and the owner
// found his .env in one copy of a project and not in the other. His decision:
// this sync runs between his own machines and his own server, transport is
// encrypted, the backup is encrypted -- so everything in a project travels
// with it. (The GitHub safety net is a different matter: GitHub is a third
// party, and safety-net-push.mjs keeps secrets out of what it pushes.)

// ====================================================== 1. The git database
// .git is deliberately NOT synced. Git writes many small files in a fixed
// order when it tidies up; a sync landing in the middle leaves the directory
// broken. A travelling index.lock also freezes the repository on the other
// machine. The source travels, the history does not -- every machine builds
// its own .git from the remote and keeps it in line with it
// (awg-tools/git-here.mjs, docs/notes/git-on-every-computer.md).
.git
.git/**

// ================================================ 2. Re-installable
// Third-party packages and build output. Rebuilt from the source, and the
// lion's share of the file count: 2,089,916 of 2,377,476 files.
node_modules
.next
.turbo
.nuxt
.svelte-kit
.angular
dist
build
out
target
bin/Debug
bin/Release
obj
__pycache__
*.pyc
venv
.venv
env
.tox
.mypy_cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.cache
.parcel-cache
.gradle
.m2
Pods
.pnpm-store
.yarn/cache
.yarn/unplugged
coverage
.nyc_output
DerivedData

// ================================================ 2b. Tool scratch
// Claude Code checks a repository out a SECOND time under
// .claude/worktrees/agent-<id> for every agent it isolates, and removes the
// checkout when the agent is done: a thousand files that come and go. Its
// .git is a file pointing into the main repository's .git, which is not
// synced, so on any other machine the checkout would be dead on arrival. On
// 2026-09-02 three of them vanishing at once read as a delete storm and cut
// the desktop off from the hub.
.claude/worktrees
// The same for the worktrees an editor session makes by hand next to a
// project, under Projekte/wt/<name>: a full checkout each, a .git file
// pointing into a repository whose .git does not travel. On 2026-09-02 four
// of them being removed (5,489 files) paused the desktop for thirteen hours.
Projekte/wt
// A running program's own live files. These are the five that failed on the
// hub every single hour and were the whole of its "did not get through" count
// on 2026-09-05: a media centre under test writes its log and its scratch
// while it runs, so what leaves the machine is a torn half of a file, and the
// receiving side rejects it. Named narrowly on purpose - "temp" and "*.lock"
// on their own would take yarn.lock and Cargo.lock with them, and those are
// work.
(?d)kodi*/temp
(?d)kodi*/userdata/temp
(?d)recordings/manager.lock
// A running program's lock file in its runtime folder: held for as long as
// the program runs, so the sync can never read it (Windows error 33, "another
// process has locked a portion of the file"), and arriving on another
// computer it could make the same program there believe a copy of it is
// already running - git's index.lock again (section 1). On 2026-10-07 a video
// converter's Projekte/DVDtoMP4/runtime/web-worker.lock kept the PC's status
// window at "1 file not saved yet" all day. Only inside a folder named
// runtime: yarn.lock and Cargo.lock sit at a project's root and are work.
(?d)runtime/*.lock
// The same media centre's settings, when it runs in a container with the
// project's kodi-profile folder as its data folder (MoviePilot's
// docker-compose.kodi.yml): it rewrites these two files every second with the
// same content, only the time changes. Measured 2026-09-29: 14,400 new
// versions of each in a day, the largest steady load the hub had, and every
// one travelled to every computer. Only the containers read them.
(?d)kodi*/userdata/guisettings.xml
(?d)kodi*/userdata/profiles.xml
// AWG's own gateway writes app.log and error.log beside its source whenever it
// or its tests run (metamcp/apps/backend/src/utils/logger.ts; git ignores
// them). Measured 2026-09-26: rewritten on every test run, 200 KB each, and
// every write travelled to every computer. Named by path on purpose - other
// projects share logs between computers deliberately (MoviePilot's
// live-debug-logs), so "*.log" everywhere would break them.
(?d)/Github/StardawnAI/AI-Working-Ground/metamcp/apps/backend/*.log
// The knowledge-graph builder (/understand) writes its batches under
// .ua/intermediate and moves the previous run to .ua/.trash-<n>, then deletes
// both. The graph itself (.ua/knowledge-graph.json and its small companions)
// is the useful part and still travels. On 2026-09-13 three projects' runs
// deleting their scratch at once read as a delete storm and cut the desktop
// off from the hub for an hour and a half. The same for a browser test's
// scratch folder, .playwright-mcp.
(?d).ua/intermediate
(?d).ua/tmp
(?d).ua/.trash-*
(?d).understand-anything/intermediate
(?d).understand-anything/.trash-*
(?d).playwright-mcp

// ==================================================== 3. Model weights
// The lion's share of the DATA volume, measured 2026-08-06:
//   flux1-fill-dev.safetensors        22.2 GB
//   _nsfw.safetensors                 11.1 GB
//   models_t5_umt5-xxl-enc-bf16.pth   10.6 GB
//   diffusion_pytorch_model (2 parts) 18.5 GB
//   t5xxl_fp16.safetensors             9.1 GB
// The twenty largest alone came to about 104 GB of 129 GB. All of them can be
// downloaded again -- and none of them ever changes.
*.safetensors
*.ckpt
*.pt
*.pth
*.onnx
*.gguf
*.msgpack
*.h5
*.tflite
*.pb
*.bin.gz
*.npz
checkpoints

// Disk images and large binaries
*.iso
*.vmdk
*.vdi
*.vhdx
*.qcow2

// Crash dumps. Only useful on the machine that crashed.
CrashDumps
*.mdmp
*.dmp

// Remains of aborted transfers. Sat on the hub as .syncthing.*.tmp at 4.7 GB
// each, never finishing.
.syncthing.*
*.incomplete
*.partial

// ============================================================ 4. System noise
(?d)desktop.ini
(?d)Thumbs.db
(?d)ehthumbs.db
(?d)$RECYCLE.BIN
(?d).DS_Store
(?d)._*
(?d).Spotlight-V100
(?d).Trashes
(?d)*.tmp
(?d)~$*
(?d).~lock.*
// A CONFLICT COPY IS WORK, SO IT IS NOT EXCLUDED HERE ANY MORE (2026-09-04).
//
// This line used to read "(?d)*.sync-conflict-*". Two mistakes in one pattern.
// Being an ignore rule it kept conflict copies OFF the sync, so the losing side
// of a conflict existed on exactly one machine. And "(?d)" tells Syncthing the
// file may be DELETED when it is in the way - so the only surviving copy of
// somebody's edit was both unreachable and disposable.
//
// A conflict copy is created precisely when two machines changed the same file
// and Syncthing had to choose. The copy is the half it did not choose. That is
// the definition of work, and the founder's rule is that an ignore list keeps
// out what disturbs the sync and never what is work. So they travel now, and
// the hub reports them - git makes you resolve a conflict, and until this
// changed nobody was even told one had happened.

// ============================================= 5. Syncthing's own folders
// At the root Syncthing handles them itself, DEEPER in the tree it does not:
// when a folder is moved, its version store would otherwise travel as
// ordinary content and be sent straight back as payload.
.stversions
.stfolder

vendor
